- Goal: close the three gaps that made ANY outside exposure unsafe.

## What was actually wrong (audited, not assumed)

1. **The most expensive route in the product had no metering at all.** One
   focused run is ~8 model calls plus bounded retrieval and takes 6-9 minutes
   live. Meanwhile the far cheaper pure-computation simulation-run route already
   had a complete limiter (`app/simulations/run_policy.py`). AGENTS.md section 11
   requires Postgres-backed limits for "login, high cost Run, connectors and
   uploads" — the high-cost-Run half was simply missing. On a public origin this
   is unbounded model/retrieval spend by anyone.
2. **`POST /api/auth/guest` had no metering.** Only login did. One address could
   mint unlimited guest workspaces, and each guest workspace carries its own run
   budget — an amplifier for (1).
3. **`app.main` had no middleware whatsoever**, so not one response carried a
   security header (nosniff / frame-ancestors / referrer-policy / COOP / CORP).
   Section 12 requires a configurable HTTPS entry point and section 14's
   security gate lists security response headers.

## Delivered

- `app/analyses/run_policy.py` (NEW): `AnalysisRunRateLimiter` with two
  independent fail-closed dimensions — per (workspace, user) burst 5 / 60 min,
  and per workspace rolling day 20 / 24 h. Storage reuses the migrated
  `login_rate_buckets` sliding window under dedicated SHA-256 digest dimensions
  (`analysisrun:` / `analysisrunday:`), so no raw workspace/user id is written to
  the limiter store and keys cannot collide with login or simulation buckets.
  No migration, no new dependency.
- `app/analyses/routes.py`: the gate runs BEFORE the handler reads the body or
  touches the charter. A 429 cannot consume an Idempotency-Key (only
  `create_queued_run` writes the idempotency record, and a 429 never reaches it).
- `app/auth/guest.py`: the CREATE path (not the free reuse path) is metered
  through the shipped `LoginRateLimiter`, with a guest-specific second dimension
  — exactly the pattern invite redemption already uses.
- `app/security/headers.py` (NEW) + `app/main.py`: pure-ASGI
  `SecurityHeadersMiddleware` adding nosniff, `X-Frame-Options: DENY`,
  `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`, COOP, CORP, a minimal `Permissions-Policy` and
  `CSP default-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'`. It never buffers a body, so
  SSE passes through untouched, and it never overwrites a header a route already
  set. **HSTS is opt-in** via `SECURITY_HSTS_ENABLED` so a plain-HTTP dev server
  cannot poison the developer's browser for the whole domain. No CORS middleware
  on purpose: the deployment contract is a single origin (web proxies `/api`),
  so permissive CORS would widen the surface rather than close a gap.

